# November 17, 2026: Rhode Island, third pass. Ruins and traces reached on foot: lost ski areas, mill ruins in town
# parks, land-trust and Audubon preserves, a bridge stub and wartime remains, plus the Seabee huts from LEADS.md.
# Facts come from the linked sources only. Wikipedia articles were read as raw text (tools/wiki_raw.py); town, state,
# federal, Nature Conservancy and Audubon pages were read in full through a page reader or tools/page.py; the ski
# history sites (NELSAP, New England Ski History) and the hiker and newspaper pages were read through a page reader
# that was asked for dates, remains, owner and access. Where sources disagree the text gives both; where a source is
# silent it says "not checked".
# Pins: an OpenStreetMap feature, an address lookup, a coordinate printed by the source, or a spot picked out on the
# March 2026 state leaf-off aerial and the 2022 state LiDAR, as each pin note says. Every pin was looked at on the pin
# check (research/pincheck_ri3_*.jpg) and on side-by-side aerial and LiDAR views.
from data_b import N
from data_ma import W
def L(t,u): return [t,u]
NELSAP_PT=L('NELSAP: Pinetop, Escoheag RI','https://nelsap.org/ri/pinetopri.html')
NESH_PT=L('New England Ski History: Pinetop','https://newenglandskihistory.com/RhodeIsland/pinetop.php')
ORANGE=L('RI DEM: orange in management areas during hunting seasons','https://riparks.ri.gov/press-releases/dem-reminds-public-wear-500-square-inches-solid-fluorescent-orange-during-shotgun-0')
TNC_CB=L('The Nature Conservancy: Canonchet Brook Preserve','https://www.nature.org/en-us/get-involved/how-to-help/places-we-protect/canonchet-brook-preserve/')
PLACES=[
# ---- South County and the western woods ----
N('pine-top-ski-area','Pine Top ski area','West Greenwich','risouth','amuse','ruins',41.61722,-71.77972,'site','public','hike','amusements,burned','1965–c.1980',
  'A ski area on Escoheag Hill that opened for the 1965-66 season with two T-bar lifts, snowmaking and night skiing, and advertised parking for 1,200 cars. Poor snow years hurt it in the late 1970s. When it closed is not agreed: Wikipedia and New England Ski History give about 1980, NELSAP about 1981. Vandals burned the base lodge in the early to mid 1980s. The land is now part of the Arcadia Management Area.',
  'NELSAP visitors in 2004 and 2006 found the ruins of a warming hut, snowmaking pipes at the base, concrete from a T-bar flywheel near the top of the old Falls Run slope, lift foundations at the summit and a small red building with rusted breaker boxes north-west of the summit. A 2024 visitor found the base flat and grassy and the old trail cuts only a little less wooded than the forest; another reported large cement blocks on the hill. What survives now was not checked.',
  'The most popular ski hill in Rhode Island, now a hiking trail with lift footings in the woods.',
  'State land in the Arcadia Management Area (DEM). NELSAP describes a small parking area off Molasses Hill Road (also called Hazard Road), a gate across the old ski-area lot and the North-South Trail running through it to the base; the slopes are badly eroded and the base is thick with poison ivy. DEM requires orange clothing in management areas during hunting seasons. Hours were not checked.',
  [NELSAP_PT,NESH_PT,W('Escoheag Hill'),ORANGE],conf='mixed'),
N('canonchet-brook-sawmill','Canonchet Brook sawmill and farm ruins','Hopkinton','risouth','industry','ruins',41.48416,-71.74351,'area','public','hike','','1700s–1800s',
  'In the 1700s Jonathan Foster ran a large farm and sawmill on what is now the Canonchet Brook Preserve. Brooks like this one were dammed in the 1800s to drive sawmills cutting shingles, clapboards and floorboards. The Nature Conservancy calls the stone work in and around the preserve some of the best in the state.',
  'About half a mile up the yellow Canonchet Trail from Route 3: the ruins of the sawmill and the stone foundations of a barn and a farmhouse. The preserve also has a colonial stone millrace slowly falling in beside a stream, a cellar hole with straight walls and sharp corners, a small slab bridge over Canonchet Brook and walled farm lanes.',
  '',
  'Canonchet Brook Preserve, 822 acres, run by The Nature Conservancy with the Hopkinton Land Trust. Open year-round from an hour before sunrise to an hour after sunset. Main trailhead at about 750 Main Street (Route 3), Hope Valley; more parking at about 160 North Road, 175 Lawton Foster Road North and the end of Stubtown Road. The yellow and blue loop past the ruins is about 2 miles. Stay on marked trails near neighbours, dogs on leash, no bikes or horses. Archery deer hunting on part of the preserve: wear orange from September 15 to January 31. Do not climb or sit on the stone features.',
  [TNC_CB]),
N('beaver-river-grist-mill','Beaver River Preserve grist mill ruins','Richmond','risouth','industry','ruins',41.541907,-71.653631,'area','public','hike','','colonial',
  'The Nature Conservancy\'s preserve on the Beaver River, set up in the early 2000s, has the ruins of a colonial grist mill and its bypass canal on the Fox Ridge trail. The page gives no date or description beyond that. A dam on the preserve breached late in 2023 and drained a beaver pond.',
  'The ruins of the grist mill and the bypass canal, on the yellow trail from Fox Ridge Drive. What they look like was not checked.',
  '',
  'Beaver River Preserve, 266 acres, The Nature Conservancy. Open year-round from an hour before sunrise to an hour after sunset. The mill is on the Fox Ridge side: park on either side of the dead end of Fox Ridge Drive and respect the neighbours. The Hillsdale trailhead at 350 Hillsdale Road does not connect across the river. Trails can be wet and overgrown. Bowhunting October 1 to January 31: wear an orange hat or vest. Dogs on leash; no bikes or horses.',
  [L('The Nature Conservancy: Beaver River Preserve','https://www.nature.org/en-us/get-involved/how-to-help/places-we-protect/beaver-river-preserve')]),
# ---- Narragansett Bay, Newport and the East Bay ----
N('tillinghast-mill-site','Tillinghast Mill site (Mount Hope Factory)','East Greenwich','ribay','industry','ruins',41.62895,-71.5055,'exact','public','walk','','1812–after 1905',
  'A cotton mill, also called the Mount Hope Factory, most likely begun by Allin and Joseph Joslyn Tillinghast. Wikipedia gives 1812 and a GoLocalProv walk says about 1813. It spun cotton yarn and later made printed cloth, carpet and twine. At its height there were 25 worker houses, the waterworks, a wooden mill and a four-storey factory. It ran until at least 1905. The town bought the site in 1969 and it was listed on the National Register in 1988.',
  'Foundations of the mill, a boiler room, a restroom, bridge footings, the waterworks and several house sites, along the stream below the mill pond. The 2022 state LiDAR shows a row of rectangular foundations along the stream at the pin.',
  '',
  'Frenchtown Park, owned by the Town of East Greenwich, behind the Parks Department building at 1127 Frenchtown Road, with a parking lot. Follow the yellow Cotton Mill Trail, or the path along the stream from the gap in the bridge railing at the pond. Hours were not checked.',
  [W('Tillinghast Mill Site'),L('GoLocalProv: Frenchtown Park walk','https://www.golocalprov.com/news/our-environment-secret-frenchtown-park-by-scott-turner'),L('Explore RI: Frenchtown Park trail map','https://exploreri.org/trailmaps/Frenchtown_Trails.pdf'),L('Birding Hotspots: Frenchtown Park','https://birdinghotspots.org/hotspot/L6530163')],
  th=[41.62599,-71.5063],thn='Frenchtown Park lot, 1127 Frenchtown Road'),
N('old-jamestown-bridge-abutment','Old Jamestown Bridge west abutment','North Kingstown','ribay','landmark','traces',41.5297,-71.4156,'site','view','walk','','1940–1992',
  'A two-lane steel cantilever bridge across the West Passage to Conanicut Island. It opened on July 27, 1940 with a 90-cent toll and closed to traffic on October 8, 1992, when the new Jamestown Verrazzano Bridge took over. The main span was blown up on April 18, 2006. The western trestle was left for a fishing pier, but in 2010 the state paid to have its last 1,600 feet taken down instead.',
  'Wikipedia says the western abutment and the dead-end roadbed leading to it remain. The 2022 LiDAR shows the old roadbed as a raised strip just south of the new bridge, ending at the shore at the pin. The March 2026 aerial shows vehicles and heaps of material on the cleared strip.',
  'The stub of the 1940 bridge, beside the one that replaced it.',
  'State land. A state law (R.I. Gen. Laws 24-12-51.1) tells the transportation and environment departments to keep the land by the old bridge on the North Kingstown side as a free public parking and shore access site, which they may close from sunset to sunrise. Whether it is open, signed or fenced today was not checked, and the aerial suggests work going on: look from the shore until you know.',
  [W('Jamestown Bridge'),L('RIDOT, October 2010: removal of the remaining trestle','https://ri.gov/press/view/12374'),L('R.I. Gen. Laws 24-12-51.1: public fishing area','https://webserver.rilegislature.gov/statutes/TITLE24/24-12/24-12-51.1.htm')],conf='mixed'),
N('seabee-museum-quonset-huts','Seabee Museum Quonset huts (Camp Endicott)','North Kingstown','ribay','fort','standing',41.60676,-71.4494,'site','public','drive','forts','1942–',
  'Camp Endicott at Davisville trained more than 100,000 Navy Seabees in the Second World War, and the Quonset hut was designed and made here. A seven-acre piece of the camp with 17 huts was listed on the National Register in 1978 and later mostly demolished. Former Seabees founded the Seabee Museum and Memorial Park on the old base; its new museum and store opened on May 30, 2015.',
  'The concrete Chapel-in-the-Pines (rededicated in October 2023), two ammunition bunkers and seven Quonset huts painted in camouflage. Where the huts came from is told two ways: Wikipedia says they were saved from Camp Endicott, the museum says they were found at Quonset Point and round New England and moved here.',
  '',
  'Seabee Museum and Memorial Park, a non-profit museum at 21 Iafrate Way, on about 6 acres of the old base that its founders asked the state to lease or transfer to them (which was done is not stated). Opening days and hours were not checked: see the museum\'s own site before going.',
  [W('Camp Endicott'),W('Seabee Museum and Memorial Park'),L('Seabee Museum: overview','https://seabeesmuseum.com/?p=10')],conf='mixed'),
N('sachuest-point-military-remains','Sachuest Point Navy range and radar remains','Middletown','ribay','fort','traces',41.4775,-71.243,'area','public','walk','forts','1917–1973',
  'Two guns were set up here in the First World War against U-boats and removed in 1919. In the Second World War the Navy built a rifle range and a radio station, and the Army built three fire control towers disguised as a farmhouse, a barn and a silo, and a radar disguised as a water tower. The radar came down in 1948, the radio station in 1973 and the towers by about 1985. The Fish and Wildlife Service says 107 acres in the middle of the point were an Army coastal defence site and Navy firing range from 1945 to 1973. The refuge began in 1970.',
  'The two accounts differ: Wikipedia says the remains of the rifle range still exist, and the New England defences site says the foundations of the radar buildings are the only visible remains. The 2022 LiDAR shows long straight banks and two round rings in the scrub south of the visitor centre; no source says what they are.',
  '',
  'Sachuest Point National Wildlife Refuge (U.S. Fish and Wildlife Service), 769 Sachuest Point Road. Trails open sunrise to sunset; visitor centre daily 10 to 3. No dogs or other pets, no bikes on the trails. The lot fills on busy days.',
  [W('Sachuest Point National Wildlife Refuge'),L('New England Defenses: Sachuest Point','https://nedefenses.com/index.php/Sachuest_Point_MR'),L('U.S. Fish and Wildlife Service: about Sachuest Point','https://fws.gov/refuge/sachuest-point/about-us'),L('U.S. Fish and Wildlife Service: visit Sachuest Point','https://www.fws.gov/refuge/sachuest-point/visit-us')],
  conf='mixed',th=[41.47962,-71.24401],thn='Refuge visitor centre lot'),
N('simmons-mill-pond-ruins','Simmons Mill Pond mill race and farm site','Little Compton','ribay','industry','traces',41.53964,-71.14867,'area','public','walk','','c.1750',
  'Cold Brook was dammed about 1750 to drive a grist mill, which flooded the lowland and made the wetlands round Simmons Mill Pond. The state bought the land from the Chace family in 1995 and runs it as a management area of more than 500 acres. Who owned the mill is not given.',
  'Near the pond, the stone-walled raceway of the mill. On the Amy Hart Path, a farm site with an old well, a barn foundation and stone walls round former pastures. Described in 2016; not checked since.',
  '',
  'Simmons Mill Pond Management Area, state land. Main lot on Cold Brook Road near Long Highway; a smaller lot on John Dyer Road. A half-mile walk down to the pond, a one-mile farm loop and the Amy Hart Loop. Open to hunting in season: wear orange.',
  [L('RI Wild Plant Society: Simmons Mill Pond Management Area','https://riwps.org/on-the-trail/simmons-mill-pond-management-area-little-compton-ri/'),ORANGE],conf='mixed'),
# ---- Providence, the Blackstone Valley and the western hills ----
N('valley-falls-mill-ruins','Valley Falls Company mill ruins (Heritage Park)','Cumberland','riprov','industry','ruins',41.9,-71.38933,'site','public','walk','','1818–1930s',
  'Mills stood at Valley Falls from early in the 1800s: the town\'s plan says a cotton mill was probably here about 1813, the walking tour that the first mill opened in 1818. Oliver Chace bought the mills on both banks in 1839 and founded the Valley Falls Company, a forerunner of Berkshire Hathaway. Work stopped with the Depression and the owners pulled the mill down to save property tax: in 1934 says the tour, in the early 1930s says the plan. The town got the land from the state in 1988 and opened the park in 1993.',
  'Granite foundations you can walk across on ramps and bridges, open canals and sluices that fed the turbines, arches, the gear wheels of the gatehouse by the river, a tall concrete foundation fragment by the parking area, and the Shea Bridge, an 1880s iron truss brought here from Smithfield in 1994. The town\'s 2020 plan mentions poison ivy, graffiti and a plan to cut the tall fragment down.',
  'The ruins of the mill that started Berkshire Hathaway, turned into a park.',
  'Valley Falls Heritage Park, Town of Cumberland, 45 Broad Street. Park at the Town Hall lot or along Broad Street; restrooms in Town Hall. The Blackstone River Bikeway passes the park. Hours were not checked.',
  [W('Valley Falls Company'),L('Blackstone Heritage Corridor: Valley Falls walking tour','https://blackstoneheritagecorridor.org/wp-content/uploads/2016/02/Valley-Falls-Walking-Tour.pdf'),L('Town of Cumberland: Heritage Park plan 2020-2025','https://www.cumberlandri.gov/DocumentCenter/View/316/Heritage-Park-PDF'),L('SAH Archipedia: Valley Falls mill ruins','https://sah-archipedia.org/node/19028')],conf='mixed'),
N('diamond-hill-ski-area','Diamond Hill ski area','Cumberland','riprov','amuse','traces',42.0005,-71.416,'area','public','hike','amusements','c.1940–1980s',
  'In 1935 three men offered 235 acres of Diamond Hill to the state, and ski trails were cut soon after. The state ran the ski area, then leased it out from 1965-66. It had rope tows, a T-bar from 1966 and two double chairlifts from 1970. Ski Valley opened on the back of the hill for 1961-62. The years are told differently: Wikipedia gives 1939 to 1981 for Ski Valley and the mid-1960s to mid-1980s for Diamond Hill, New England Ski History has Diamond Hill closing before 1982 and Ski Valley after 1984-85, and the town says skiing ran from 1939 into the 1980s. The town took the park over in 1997.',
  'Bottom footings of the double chair were photographed in the base area in 2010. Nothing newer was checked.',
  'Rhode Island\'s state-run ski hill, now a town park with chairlift footings in the grass.',
  'Diamond Hill Park, Town of Cumberland, 4097 Diamond Hill Road, 0.6 mile south of Route 121; large lot on the road. 3.8 miles of marked trails, and the start of the 30-mile Warner Trail. Hours were not checked.',
  [W('Diamond Hill (Cumberland, Rhode Island)'),L('New England Ski History: Diamond Hill','https://newenglandskihistory.com/RhodeIsland/diamondhill.php'),L('Town of Cumberland: Diamond Hill Town Park','https://www.cumberlandri.gov/303/Diamond-Hill-PDF')],
  conf='mixed',th=[42.00199,-71.41877],thn='Diamond Hill Park lot, 4097 Diamond Hill Road'),
N('hayfield-mill-site-foster','Hayfield mill site on the Ponagansett','Foster','riprov','industry','ruins',41.8325,-71.7288,'area','public','hike','','',
  'An old mill site at the south end of the Foster Land Trust\'s Hayfield Property, on the Ponagansett River. Neither the town nor the walk report found gives a date or says what the mill made; the walk report guessed a grist or sawmill tied to the Hopkins Mills village nearby.',
  'A 2023 walk report found the entrance of the mill race, some walls and a cellar hole or two at the end of the paths, and placed them on Winsor Brook; the town puts the mill on the Ponagansett River. The 2022 LiDAR shows a straight-sided channel near the pin, which may be the race; not confirmed.',
  '',
  'Hayfield Property, 109 acres, Foster Land Trust, with parking at pole 54 Winsor Road off Route 101. The yellow Hayfield Trail runs south to the mill site. The town says the mill stands on State of Rhode Island land just past the land-trust line, and the trail map marks DEM land there. Trails open to hikers, bikes and horses. Hours were not checked.',
  [L('Town of Foster: Hayfield Property trails','https://www.townoffoster.com/node/14053'),L('Foster Land Trust trail map (Explore RI)','https://exploreri.org/trailmaps/Hayfield_trail_map.pdf'),L('Land in Trust: Hayfield Property, 2023','https://landintrust.blogspot.com/2023/04/hayfield-property.html')],conf='mixed'),
N('parker-woodland-ruins','Parker Woodland sawmill and farm ruins','Coventry','riprov','industry','ruins',41.716389,-71.698167,'area','public','hike','','1700s–1800s',
  'Farmers of the late 1700s and the 1800s worked this rocky land on the Coventry-Foster line. Brown University students dug a semicircular mound here in 1983 and found it was a charcoal pit. The land is now an 860-acre Audubon refuge.',
  'From Parking Lot 2, the yellow trail passes an unfinished stone foundation in a ledge. Along Turkey Meadow Brook, on the south edge of the refuge, are the remains of a sawmill: stone walls, a sluiceway and perhaps a wheel pit. Elsewhere are a farm complex with foundations, chimneys, a wellhead, walled pens and lanes, a small quarry, a three-sided barn foundation and stone cairns of unknown age. Seen on a December 2023 newspaper walk.',
  '',
  'George B. Parker Woodland Wildlife Refuge, Audubon Society of Rhode Island. Open sunrise to sunset. Lot 1 is by the house at 1670 Maple Valley Road; Lot 2 is half a mile east on the same road. No dogs, bikes beyond the lot, hunting, collecting or geocaching. 7 miles of trails, moderate to hard.',
  [L('Audubon Society of Rhode Island: Parker Woodland','https://asri.org/hike/wildliferefuges/george-b-parker-woodland-wildlife-refuge.html'),L('Providence Journal, December 2023: Parker Woodland walk','https://www.pressreader.com/usa/the-providence-journal/20231210/281878713154103')]),
]
BATCH={'key':'ri3','label':'Rhode Island, third pass: ski hills, mill ruins and wartime traces','added':'2026-11-17 05:40'}
PIN_NOTES={
 'pine-top-ski-area':'Pin is the base-area coordinate printed on the NELSAP page (41.37.02N 71.46.47W), beside Molasses Hill Road. On the March 2026 aerial it falls in woods just south of a large open sandy clearing, probably the old lot; the warming hut was not picked out.',
 'canonchet-brook-sawmill':'Pin is an address lookup of 750 Main Street (Route 3), which TNC gives as the main trailhead; on the aerial it falls on Route 3 beside houses and a greenhouse yard, and the trailhead lot was not picked out. The sawmill ruins are about half a mile up the yellow trail and were not found on the aerial or LiDAR, so the pin marks only the stretch of road where the trail starts.',
 'beaver-river-grist-mill':'Pin is the coordinate The Nature Conservancy gives for the preserve. On the aerial it falls at the end of a residential road among houses, which fits the Fox Ridge Drive trailhead. The grist mill is farther in on the trail and was not picked out on the aerial or LiDAR.',
 'tillinghast-mill-site':'Pin is the OpenStreetMap "Tillinghast Mill Ruins" feature. On the 2022 LiDAR it falls on a row of rectangular foundations along the stream below the mill pond; under bare trees on the aerial.',
 'old-jamestown-bridge-abutment':'Pin is the shore end of the old roadbed, picked out on the 2022 LiDAR as a raised strip just south of the new bridge; on the aerial it falls on the bank where the strip meets the beach. The Wikipedia coordinate for the bridge is mid-channel.',
 'seabee-museum-quonset-huts':'Pin is the coordinate in the Wikipedia article on the museum; an address lookup of 21 Iafrate Way falls within 10 m.',
 'sachuest-point-military-remains':'Pin is in the middle of the straight banks the 2022 LiDAR shows south of the visitor centre. No source places the range or the radar foundations, so treat it as the vicinity.',
 'simmons-mill-pond-ruins':'Pin is an address lookup of Simmons Mill Pond (the pond itself). The raceway and the farm site were not picked out.',
 'valley-falls-mill-ruins':'Pin is the OpenStreetMap feature for Valley Falls Heritage Park. On the aerial and LiDAR it falls on the ruined foundations between Broad Street and the river.',
 'diamond-hill-ski-area':'An address lookup of Diamond Hill Park fell on house lots west of Diamond Hill Road, so the pin was moved about 300 m east, across the road, the playing field and the pond, to the wooded foot of the slope, which the public-land map shows inside Diamond Hill Park. The lift footings were not picked out.',
 'hayfield-mill-site-foster':'Pin is a straight-sided channel seen on the 2022 LiDAR near the river at the south end of the property. It may be the race; the mill itself was not picked out.',
 'parker-woodland-ruins':'Pin is the GPS point the Audubon Society prints for the refuge. On the aerial it falls by the house at 1670 Maple Valley Road, beside which Lot 1 lies. The ruins are spread over 860 acres; the sawmill is on Turkey Meadow Brook at the south edge and was not picked out.',
}
